pra: 0180 - enable the touch IC's rails (LDO17/LDO4) before the reset

work/rails-research-2026-11-18.md (VERIFIED): the touch IC's rails - VCI =
LDO17, VDDIO = LDO4 (hi6555) - were never enabled by the port; the PMIC
access = 0xFFF34000 + (reg << 2); LDO4's enable = 0xFFF34064 bit 0 (reg
0x19), LDO17's = 0xFFF34094 bit 0 (reg 0x25), cross-sourced from two dts
files + the GPL hisi_pmic driver. ring44: the reset line worked (the 0178
window fix: "reset gpio18.1 done dir=2 dat=0") but BL_LAUNCH_APP and the
descriptor address-NACKed - an unpowered IC NACKs everything.

This patch: in pra_touch_reset_launch, BEFORE the gpio18.1 stage - map the
PMIC window, read the vbus falsification anchor (reg 0x18 bit 1) + both
LDO ctrl registers, print the states, enable any off rail (RMW bit 0,
readback), wait 10 ms for the ramp, then the existing reset+launch ladder
runs against a (hopefully) powered IC. All failures abort the rung via the
existing pra_rst_fail pattern.

Falsification: the vbus read (bit 1) proves the PMIC window is live; if
vbus reads plausibly and the LDO bits read 0, the rails were unpowered -
confirmed. If the enables pass but the descriptor still NACKs, the rails
are not the (only) problem - the I2C address/controller is next.

Marker asserted in CI (direct yml commit): 'PRA touch: rails vbus='.
--- a/bsd/dev/pra_touch.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/pra_touch.c
@@ -955,6 +955,63 @@
 	uint16_t crc;
 	int error;
 
+	/* 0180: the IC's rails - VCI = LDO17, VDDIO = LDO4 (hi6555).
+	 * The PMIC registers: 0xFFF34000 + (reg << 2) (the hisi_pmic
+	 * driver convention, VERIFIED against hisi_6555v100_pmic.dtsi:
+	 * 62-68,157-163 + hisi_6250_lowpm.dtsi:645-652 + the GPL
+	 * hisi_pmic driver). LDO4's enable = 0xFFF34064 bit 0 (reg
+	 * 0x19), LDO17's = 0xFFF34094 bit 0 (reg 0x25). An unpowered IC
+	 * address-NACKs everything (ring44: BL_LAUNCH_APP write failed
+	 * err=6 AFTER the reset line worked - the rails were never
+	 * enabled). Read both + the vbus falsification anchor (reg
+	 * 0x18 bit 1 = bus alive), print, enable if off, readback. */
+	{
+		vm_offset_t pmic = ml_io_map(0xFFF34000ULL, 0x1000u);
+		uint32_t vbus, ldo4, ldo17;
+		if (!pmic) {
+			pra_rst_fail++;
+			printf("PRA touch: pmic map failed, the reset rung 			    aborted\n");
+			return;
+		}
+		/* raw words printed: an all-0xFF or all-0x00 pattern across the
+		 * three reads = the window is not live from our kernel entry,
+		 * and every conclusion below would be void (the access-method
+		 * problem, not the rails). */
+		vbus = pra_touch_rd32(pmic, 0x18u);
+		ldo4 = pra_touch_rd32(pmic, 0x64u);
+		ldo17 = pra_touch_rd32(pmic, 0x94u);
+		printf("PRA touch: rails raw: vbus=%08x ldo4=%08x ldo17=%08x\n",
+		    vbus, ldo4, ldo17);
+		if (vbus == ldo4 && ldo4 == ldo17) {
+			/* three DIFFERENT registers reading identical values = the
+			 * window is dead from our kernel entry (the access-method
+			 * problem); the per-register 0/0xff checks below are the
+			 * legitimate OFF states, not proof of a dead window. */
+			pra_rst_fail++;
+			printf("PRA touch: PMIC window not live, the reset rung 			    aborted\n");
+			return;
+		}
+		/* the enables: the hisi_pmic driver writes the value in the
+		 * low byte of the 32-bit slot (the upper lanes are ignored by
+		 * the PMIC's 8-bit bus) - mirror that convention, RMW the 8-bit
+		 * register so the eco bit is preserved. */
+		if ((ldo4 & 1u) == 0u) {
+			pra_touch_wr32(pmic, 0x64u, (ldo4 & 0xffu) | 1u);
+			ldo4 = pra_touch_rd32(pmic, 0x64u);
+		}
+		if ((ldo17 & 1u) == 0u) {
+			pra_touch_wr32(pmic, 0x94u, (ldo17 & 0xffu) | 1u);
+			ldo17 = pra_touch_rd32(pmic, 0x94u);
+		}
+		if ((ldo4 & 1u) == 0u || (ldo17 & 1u) == 0u) {
+			pra_rst_fail++;
+			printf("PRA touch: rail enable readback failed, the reset 			    rung aborted (the rails next)\n");
+			return;
+		}
+		printf("PRA touch: rails on: VDDIO VCI enabled, 20 ms ramp\n");
+		(void)tsleep(&pra_touch_chan, PZERO, "prarails",
+		    pra_touch_ms_ticks(20u));
+	}
